  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 321 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nSunday, an upper-level ridge will amplify across the western United \nStates while a trough remains across the Hudson Bay region. This \nwill place the forecast area beneath northwesterly flow aloft, with \nweak cold air advection and increasing subsidence across the region. \nAt the surface, high pressure will build southward from southern \nCanada on the backside of the trough and along the eastern periphery \nof the western ridge. This should result in lighter northerly winds, \nclearing skies, and cooler temperatures. Highs Sunday should remain \nin the upper 60s to lower 70s, with overnight lows falling into the \nmiddle to upper 40s.\n\nMonday, the upper-level ridge will begin to flatten across the \nwestern United States as a weak disturbance moves through the \nnorthwestern United States. Ahead of this wave, surface low pressure \nwill deepen along the lee of the Rockies, resulting in a \nstrengthening pressure gradient and increasing southerly flow across \nthe region. Wind gusts Monday afternoon may exceed 40 mph across \nportions of the Sandhills into north-central Nebraska. Increasing \nsoutherly flow and warm air advection will also support warmer \ntemperatures, with highs reaching the lower to middle 70s across \nmost of the area and lower 80s across western Nebraska. Monday night \nlows should remain in the upper 40s across eastern portions of the \narea and lower 50s elsewhere.\n\nTuesday into midweek, upper-level ridging will remain the dominant \nfeature across the western United States, maintaining generally \nwesterly to northwesterly flow aloft across the central Plains. \nContinued height rises and warm temperatures aloft should support \nabove-normal temperatures, with highs generally in the upper 70s to \nlower 80s through the middle of the week. Looking at the broader \npattern, ensemble guidance continues to favor persistent ridging \nacross the western United States, with troughing remaining near the \nGulf of Alaska and the stronger upper-level flow displaced north of \nthe region. The positive PNA, EPO, and AO regimes also remain \nsupportive of the broader pattern currently in place. As a result, \nthere is increasing confidence that above-normal temperatures and \ngenerally dry conditions will persist through much of next week. \nWhile individual disturbances may occasionally move through the \nnorthwesterly flow and briefly suppress the ridge, there is \ncurrently little indication of a more substantial pattern change \nthrough at least late next week.",
